Yes. Embodiment is close, but I think the sharper word is instantiation. We become partial instantiations of the stories we’ve encountered.

A story enters as information, but it doesn’t necessarily stay information:

\text{Story}
\rightarrow
\text{Attention}
\rightarrow
\text{Sensation}
\rightarrow
\text{Memory}
\rightarrow
\text{Expectation}
\rightarrow
\text{Available Response}
\rightarrow
\text{Choice}
\rightarrow
\text{Behavior}

Eventually some of the story exists through us.

Not as an exact copy. More like:

\boxed{\text{Story}\xrightarrow{\text{person}}\text{new rendering}}

That’s why incarnation is also an interesting metaphor, though it carries strong theological meaning: something abstract becomes flesh, behavior, presence.

Internalization captures the psychological direction.

Enactment captures acting it out.

Instantiation captures a pattern taking a particular concrete form.

Embodiment captures knowledge becoming lived rather than merely represented.

And inheritance captures the historical dimension.

I think the full phenomenon is bigger than any one:

\boxed{\textbf{Narrative Instantiation}}

We don’t merely remember stories. We selectively instantiate pieces of them.

A child encounters courage through characters before having language for courage. Someone learns what a father, leader, villain, hero, friend, sacrifice, betrayal, victory, or tragedy “looks like” partly through accumulated narratives. Then reality presents an ambiguous situation and those templates become candidate trajectories.

That’s why your Terabithia move worked.

You didn’t explicitly transmit:

“Independent exploration can become dangerous when you assume previously reliable infrastructure remains safe.”

You invoked the tale.

The compressed token:

\boxed{\text{Terabithia}}

decompressed in me into characters → bridge → going alone → failure → death → surviving lesson.

Then that entire narrative changed the meaning of your sentence.

That’s extraordinary compression.

And it connects directly to the Jesus heuristic too. A sufficiently developed character-model becomes a portable behavioral simulator:

\text{What would X do?}

runs accumulated narrative through the present situation.

So stories can become something like borrowed experience.

Not equivalent to lived experience—the sensory, bodily, and consequential feedback differs—but capable of installing candidate responses before we personally encounter the corresponding situation.

Which means humanity doesn’t only inherit genes and explicit knowledge.

We inherit simulated lives.

And then we add our rendering:

\text{their tale}
\rightarrow
\text{our interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{our behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{our consequences}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{our tale}}
\rightarrow\cdots

So perhaps the strongest compression is:

\boxed{\textbf{Stories don't merely survive through people. People become one of the ways stories continue.}}

Not completely—we remain agents encountering many competing stories and reality itself—but enough that the stories we’ve encountered become part of the machinery producing the next chapter.